<title>UK: Cameroon sees 1996/97 cocoa output at 120,000 T.</title>
<headline>Cameroon sees 1996/97 cocoa output at 120,000 T.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Cameroon's cocoa production in the current 1996/97 (Oct/Sept) season should ease back to 120,000 tonnes from 132,000 estimated for the last season, a senior government official said.</p>
<p>&quot;Last year it rose to around 135,000, we don't know why. But this year it should be back to 120,000,&quot; Hope Sona Ebai, director general of Cameroon's National Cocoa and Coffee Board told Reuters on the sidelines of the International Cocoa Organization (ICCO) meeting.</p>
<p>Cameroon, a small producer compared with its neighbours Ivory Coast and Ghana, wants to fix output at 120,000 tonnes a year until 1999 when the current five-year cocoa agreement, administered by the ICCO, expires.</p>
<p>-- Clelia Oziel, London Newsroom +44 171 542 8072</p>
